			<div class="content" id="post-content-3129198">
									

<p>If there's spelling or grammar issues, point them out. Or even fix them yourself, I don't mind.</p>
<p>The 'weird unredaction' is important in the context of the article - the Soviet Union was collapsing in 1991. It's relevant to show that this attempted false-flag attack occurred prior to that.</p>
<p>Here's the parts where SCP-XXXX-2 is designated, and the source of SCP-XXXX-1 instances is described:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Groups of SCP-2326-1 in flight strength will spontaneously appear over the North Atlantic at 16,000&#160;m. Such appearances are termed 2326-Tango events. Instances will attempt to fly toward Warsaw Pact industrial centers, major military bases, and other strategic locations. It is presumed that instances of SCP-2326-1 will attempt to destroy these cities with their payloads</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Site 2326-A was originally a control facility carefully disguised as a radar site. At some point after [REDACTED], it fell into disrepair until it was restored as a Foundation site after the discovery of its association with 2326-Tango events from rediscovered GRU 'P' Division documents. It contains the remains of a previously unknown variation of the Elbrus-3 computer linked to a Dnestr radar array, <strong>designated SCP-2326-2</strong>, as well as the remains of a GRU 'P' Division research site. Based on partial recovered documentation, the currently non-functioning network is the control mechanism for SCP-2326-1 instances, and currently in the active mode with no way to return the system to an inactive state. Work to restore functionality is ongoing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As for surviving instances being unable to answer questions, there's an entire interview of them not being able to respond adequately.</p>
<p>I don't understand what issue you're taking with the request for assistance - it's presumably via a dead drop or letter because both organizations typically do not cooperate - it's unofficial and clandestine.</p>

